.TH FERMI_SSO_YUBIKEY 3 "2026-09-02" "Fermi-SSO-AccessLib 0.4.0" "Python Library"
.SH NAME
fermi_sso_yubikey \- YubiKey (PIV) certificate hop for FermiSSOSession
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
.B from fermi_sso_yubikey import YubiKeyCertHop
.B from requests_fermi_sso import FermiSSOSession
.sp
.B s = FermiSSOSession(auth_method=\(dqyubikey\(dq, cert_hop=YubiKeyCertHop())
.BI "r = s.get(\(dq" URL \(dq)
.sp
.B fermi_sso_yubikey
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Fermilab's identity provider authenticates its hardware\-token adapters
(FNAL Yubikey, HSPD\-12) by client certificate on a separate mutual\-TLS
listener,
.IR pingprod.fnal.gov:9032 .
The certificate is the one in the YubiKey's PIV authentication slot (9A),
issued by FERMI Sub CA 01, and its private key never leaves the token.
Python's TLS stack can only load keys from files, so
.B FermiSSOSession
cannot perform that request itself.
.PP
.B YubiKeyCertHop
performs exactly that one request. Given the redirect the identity provider
issues after the adapter is selected, it runs
.PP
.RS
.EX
openssl s_client \-connect pingprod.fnal.gov:9032 \-servername pingprod.fnal.gov \e
    \-engine pkcs11 \-keyform engine \-key pkcs11:id=%01;type=private \e
    \-cert <9A certificate exported as PEM> \-quiet
.EE
.RE
.PP
writes the GET, with the session's cookies for the identity provider, to
the connection, and parses the raw HTTP response into a
.BR HopResponse .
The session absorbs the Set\-Cookie headers and follows the redirect back to
the application. The certificate is exported from the token with
.BR pkcs11\-tool (1),
which needs no PIN. The private\-key operation does:
.B openssl
prompts on the controlling terminal, unless a pin\-source file is given.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
An OpenSSL 3 build with engine support (Apple's
.I /usr/bin/openssl
is LibreSSL and will not do), the
.B pkcs11
engine from libp11, a PKCS#11 module for the token (libykcs11 from
yubico\-piv\-tool, or opensc\-pkcs11), and
.B pkcs11\-tool
from opensc. On macOS:
.PP
.RS
.EX
brew install openssl@3 libp11 yubico\-piv\-tool opensc ykman
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run
.B fermi_sso_yubikey
with no arguments to check all of it.
.SH CLASS
.TP
.BI YubiKeyCertHop( "openssl_bin=None, engines_dir=None, module_path=None, key_uri=None, pin_source=None, pkcs11_tool=None, cert_pem=None, timeout=180, environ=None, runner=None" )
Every argument falls back to the matching environment variable (see
ENVIRONMENT) and then to Homebrew and common Linux paths.
.RS
.TP
.B key_uri
PKCS#11 URI of the private key. Default
.BR pkcs11:id=%01;type=private ,
slot 9A. Add
.B token=...
when more than one token is present.
.TP
.B pin_source
Path of a file holding the PIV PIN. It is passed to the engine as
.B pin\-source=file:...
so openssl does not prompt. Keep it mode 600. Without it, openssl prompts.
.TP
.B cert_pem
Path of the 9A certificate as PEM. Exported from the token into a private
temporary directory when not given.
.TP
.B runner
Replacement for the subprocess call, for tests.
.RE
.SH METHODS
.TP
.BI __call__( url ", " prepared_request )
Perform the hop. Returns a
.BR HopResponse ;
raises
.B RuntimeError
when openssl produced no HTTP response, with openssl's last stderr line.
.TP
.B check()
List of
.RI ( what ", " ok ", " detail )
for each prerequisite. Reads the token's public objects; never touches the
PIN.
.TP
.B ensure_cert_pem()
Path of the exported certificate, exporting it on first use.
.TP
.BI command( host ", " port )
The argv that would be run, for inspection.
.TP
.B environment()
The subprocess environment:
.B OPENSSL_ENGINES
and
.B PKCS11_MODULE_PATH
set for the engine.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BI build_request( prepared_request ", " urlsplit_parts )
Serialize the GET as HTTP/1.1 bytes with the prepared headers, minus
Accept\-Encoding (the raw pipe cannot decompress), plus
.BR "Connection: close" .
.TP
.BI parse_http_response( raw_bytes )
Parse a status line, headers (all Set\-Cookie values kept), and a body
delimited by Content\-Length or chunked encoding, skipping any s_client
chatter before the status line.
.SH COMMAND LINE
.B fermi_sso_yubikey
prints the readiness report and the exact openssl command, and exits 0 when
everything is in place. Options:
.TP
.BI \-\-openssl " PATH" "\fR, \fP" \-\-engines\-dir " DIR" "\fR, \fP" \-\-module " PATH" "\fR, \fP" \-\-key " URI" "\fR, \fP" \-\-pin\-source " FILE"
Override the corresponding setting.
.TP
.BI \-\-export\-cert " FILE"
Also write the 9A certificate as PEM to
.IR FILE .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FERMI_SSO_OPENSSL
OpenSSL 3 binary.
.TP
.B FERMI_SSO_ENGINES_DIR
Directory holding
.IR pkcs11.dylib " or " pkcs11.so .
.TP
.B FERMI_SSO_PKCS11_MODULE
The PKCS#11 module.
.TP
.B FERMI_SSO_PKCS11_KEY
Private key URI.
.TP
.B FERMI_SSO_PIN_SOURCE
PIN file.
.TP
.B FERMI_SSO_PKCS11_TOOL
.BR pkcs11\-tool (1)
binary.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B no HTTP response over the client\-certificate connection
openssl exited without a response. Its last stderr line follows: a wrong or
missing PIN, a key the engine could not find (check the URI against
.BR "pkcs11\-tool \-O" ),
or a handshake the server refused.
.TP
.B access_denied
Reported by
.BR fermi_sso_probe (1)
when the hop succeeded at TLS level but the identity provider still refused
the adapter. The certificate was not accepted for this account.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR FermiSSOSession (3),
.BR fermi_sso_probe (1),
.BR ykman (1),
.BR pkcs11\-tool (1),
.BR openssl\-s_client (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Added for the Mu2e DocDB authentication work.
